    id: "26ec2afa5d734ee077f6",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    title: "Zambia's president to be sworn in for second term while opponent detained",
    summary: "Hakainde Hichilema swept to victory, but his main rival is now in detention charged with treason.",
    preview: "Zambia's President Hakainde Hichilema is due to be sworn in for a second five-year term on Tuesday following a disputed election and the arrest of his main opponent on treason charges. Hichilema - credited with stabilising the economy - swept to victory on 13 August with 60% of the vote, according to official results. Runner-up Brian Mundubile, who received 38%, alleged that the outcome had been tampered with. He was unable to mount a legal challenge to the result as the courts were sealed off last week on the final day a case could be filed.",
    body: "Zambia's President Hakainde Hichilema is due to be sworn in for a second five-year term on Tuesday following a disputed election and the arrest of his main opponent on treason charges.\n\nHichilema - credited with stabilising the economy - swept to victory on 13 August with 60% of the vote, according to official results. Runner-up Brian Mundubile, who received 38%, alleged that the outcome had been tampered with.\n\nHe was unable to mount a legal challenge to the result as the courts were sealed off last week on the final day a case could be filed.\n\nElection observers said that while polling day was largely peaceful, some have raised questions about the vote tallying process.\n\nEuropean Union observers said staff in many tallying centres did not record results on tally sheets as soon as they were announced and cited weak verification between digital entries and paper records.\n\nIt added that returning officers frequently paused proceedings while awaiting instructions from headquarters.\n\nThe day after the poll, security forces raided a property in the capital, Lusaka, linked to Mundubile, killing a former cabinet minister and arresting a number of opposition politicians.\n\nThe authorities accused the opposition of planning an armed insurrection, which they denied.\n\nMundubile and his running mate, Makebi Zulu, had gone into hiding. They were detained by police last week and charged at the weekend.\n\nWhen Hichilema, 64, came into office in 2021 after a landslide victory, Zambia had recently defaulted on its international debt and had an annual inflation rate of more than 24%.\n\n\"We inherited a broken-down countryâ¦ the economy was declining,\" Hichilema told the BBC in Lusaka before the election.\n\nThe former businessman negotiated a restructuring of the debt and rebuilt relations with foreign creditors. Inflation has also come down, but prices are still rising and many continue to struggle with the cost of living.\n\nHichilema now wants to build on the foundations he said he laid over the past five years.\n\nPresidents from Kenya, Botswana, Zimbabwe, Namibia and Tanzania are among several African leaders expected to attend the inauguration ceremony at the National Heroes Stadium in Lusaka.\n\nHichilema will be sworn in alongside his running mate, Mutale Nalumango.\n\nMore on Zambian politics:\n\n- Ex-Zambian minister killed in security raid on opposition\n\n- Published20 August\n\n- Stone-throwing anger unnerves Zambia's 'fix-it' president\n\n- Published27 November 2025\n\nGo to BBCAfrica.com, external for more news from the African continent.\n\nFollow us on Twitter @BBCAfrica, external, on Facebook at BBC Africa, external or on Instagram at bbcafrica, external",
    content_status: "full",
